The bug class: **fatal/actionable CLI errors resolved quietly and exited 0**, so `mythos ... && next` chains silently continued past failures. The fix has four parts plus one hermeticity pitfall.

### 1. Top-level `main().catch` maps thrown errors → `process.exit(1)` (`src/index.ts`)

Every error path funnels into one exit-code owner. `loadProject` throws `CliError("No project loaded …")` with actionable guidance; the catch maps *any* thrown error to exit 1 (previously the error was printed but the process exited 0).

```ts
async function main(argv: string[]): Promise<void> {
  const [command, ...rest] = argv;
  if (!command) { console.error("usage: mythos <command> [args]"); process.exit(1); }
  const project = loadProject(process.cwd());   // throws CliError when no .mythos/project.json
  switch (command) {
    case "batch":    return runBatch(rest);
    case "entities": return runEntities(project);
    default:
      console.error(`error: unknown command ${JSON.stringify(command)}`);
      process.exit(1);
  }
}

main(process.argv.slice(2)).catch((err: unknown) => {
  if (err instanceof CliError) console.error(`error: ${err.message}`);
  else console.error(err instanceof Error ? err.stack : String(err));
  process.exit(err instanceof CliError ? err.exitCode : 1);   // never exit 0 on failure
});
```

### 2. Each command validates args/types/options with `process.exit(1)`

Commands don't trust the dispatcher; they validate their own argv and exit 1 on bad input.

### 3. `batch.ts`: the 3 silent exit-0 paths → exit 1

These previously printed/returned quietly and exited 0. Now each exits 1 explicitly:

```ts
// path #1 — missing --yes
if (!rest.includes("--yes")) {
  console.error("error: batch submit requires --yes to confirm submission (no prompts in non-TTY runs)");
  process.exit(1);                     // was exit 0
}
// path #2 — submitted job disappears
const jobId = await submitJob(file);
if (!jobId) {
  console.error("error: submitted job disappeared before it could be tracked; retry");
  process.exit(1);                     // was exit 0
}
// path #3 — job failed / incomplete
if (status !== "completed") {
  console.error(`error: batch job ${jobId} finished ${status} — not completed`);
  process.exit(1);                     // was exit 0
}
```

### 4. Integration tests spawn `dist/index.js` via promisified `execFile` and assert `err.code === 1`

```js
const execFileP = promisify(execFile);
const CLI = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), "..", "dist", "index.js");

async function expectCliExitOne(args, opts) {
  await assert.rejects(runCli(args, opts), (err) => {
    assert.equal(err.code, 1, `expected exit code 1, got ${err.code}`);
    assert.ok(err.stderr, `expected stderr diagnostics`);
    return true;
  });
}
```

### 5. Pitfall: ambient `MYTHOS_PROJECTS_PATH` env var

A developer-shell `MYTHOS_PROJECTS_PATH` **redirects repo-root runs** to a different project, making entity-count assertions environment-dependent. Tests must be hermetic:

```js
function runCli(args, { cwd, env } = {}) {
  const cleanEnv = { ...process.env };
  delete cleanEnv.MYTHOS_PROJECTS_PATH;   // no ambient redirect
  delete cleanEnv.MYTHOS_CACHE_PATH;      // no ambient cache
  return execFileP(process.execPath, [CLI, ...args], { cwd, env: { ...cleanEnv, ...env } });
}
```
